Cistern-Stomp

Ingredients: ROCKABILLY & ROCK 'N' ROLL + RAW, GRITTY & BRUTALIST + Keyboards
Creative Catalyst: Born among rain-lashed antenna spires at an illegal rooftop rave over the drowned Megacity of Neo-Rotterdam in 2091.

Cistern-Stomp is the bastard child of frantic 1950s rockabilly shuffle and unyielding industrial concrete brutalism, completely stripped of electric guitars. The genre replaces the twangy hollow-body with modified tone-wheel organs, water-damaged electric pianos, and slammed mechanical keyboard chassis battered like rhythm artillery. Over thunderous, un-swung kick drums and metallic slapback clatter, frantic vocal hiccups collide with sub-aquatic low-end bass rumbles, turning traditional 12-bar rock 'n' roll progressions into an adrenaline-soaked dance of structural collapse.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 155–178 BPM. A hyper-caffeinated, aggressive locomotive chug that forces standard rockabilly swing into rigid, motorized 4/4 concrete pounding.
- Harmonic Palette: Stripped-down blues progressions butchered into harsh Dorian modes, frantic minor triads, and dissonant organ clusters hammered with fists rather than fingers.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Sub-Basin Hydro-Conduction: Pumping keyboard stems down 40-story flooded elevator shafts with waterproof hydrophones to capture the claustrophobic slapback of stagnant black tide-water.
  2. Chassis-Struck Key Perforation: Contact mics epoxied directly to the physical hammer assemblies of broken Rhodes and Wurlitzer keyboards, amplifying the plastic and metal mechanical impacts above the actual notes.
  3. Blown Selenium Clipping: Overdriving the vocal channel through vintage, moisture-degraded industrial power transformers until the rock 'n' roll vocal hiccups shred into clipped, square-wave spatters.
- Dynamics: Abrupt, binary shifts. Tracks pivot instantly from a solo clattering organ wire rattling in dead space to an overwhelming monolith of wall-of-sound industrial reverb and stomping iron floorboards.

Scene & Origin Lore
In the winter of 2091, as sea walls breached Neo-Rotterdam and abandoned lower districts became stagnant canals, squatters colonized the high-rise concrete terraces 500 meters in the sky. Cut off from imports and fresh instruments, sound-crews hoisted gutted Hammond organs, church harmoniums, and vintage electric pianos up emergency freight cables to rain-drowned helipads. Fueling their pirate sound systems with bootleg turbine fuel, they hybridized old maritime radio rockabilly broadcasts with the deafening industrial clatter of water pumps. The subculture—known as "Spire-Greakers"—wear oil-stained immersion waders over shredded satin bowling shirts, greasy pompadours shellacked in silicone sealant to resist torrential rains, and steel-toed combat brogues designed to hammer zinc rooftops in rhythm. Their gatherings are frantic, breathless physical workouts held under emergency floodlights just inches above the abyss.

Central Instruments: Battered Hammond M3 organs modified with distortion pots, contact-mic'd Wurlitzer 200As, piezo-rigged Rhodes pianos, analog monosynths tuned to simulate rusted upright slap-bass, kick triggers stomped directly onto corrugated steel slabs, and heavily distorted CB radio vocal mics.

For Fans Of: The Cramps, Suicide, Einstürzende Neubauten, early Jerry Lee Lewis played at double speed.

